= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the finite-dimensional setting of the preceding part, let $S$ be a <simple module>. Each action map $s\mapsto as$ is an $A$-module endomorphism because $A$ is commutative. The complex <Schur's lemma> makes every such action a scalar. Therefore every <linear subspace> of $S$ is an <submodule>; simplicity forces
$$
\boxed{\dim_{\mathbb C}S=1.}
$$
Equivalently, a simple <module> over a commutative <ring> is a quotient by a <maximal ideal>, and a finite-dimensional field extension of $\mathbb C$ is just $\mathbb C$.

The finite-dimensional context matters. If the clause is read as allowing an arbitrary commutative complex <algebra>, the unrestricted assertion is false: $A=\mathbb C(t)$ is simple as a <module> over itself and is infinite-dimensional over $\mathbb C$. This is <simple module of a finite-dimensional commutative complex algebra>. Thus **the one-dimensional conclusion uses the finite-dimensional algebra context**, or an alternative hypothesis such as finite dimensionality of the simple <module>.

