For $h\in G$, right-multiplying by $D_2(h)^{-1}$ gives
$$
D_1(h)TD_2(h)^{-1}=\sum_{g\in G}D_1(hg)B D_2(g^{-1}h^{-1}).
$$
Make the bijective substitution $k=hg$, so $g^{-1}h^{-1}=k^{-1}$. The sum becomes $\sum_kD_1(k)BD_2(k^{-1})=T$. Hence
$$
\boxed{D_1(h)T=TD_2(h)\quad(h\in G).}
$$
Thus $T$ is an <intertwining operator> from the second <group representation> to the first. This is the <Schur averaging of rectangular matrices> construction.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The preceding intertwining identity makes $T$ a homomorphism between the corresponding <simple modules>. By <Schur's lemma>, a nonzero such homomorphism would be an <isomorphism>. The representations are inequivalent, so this is impossible. Therefore
$$
\boxed{T=0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For equal irreducible <group representations>, the intertwining identity and the complex <Schur's lemma> give $T=cI_n$. Conjugation preserves <trace>, so
$$
\operatorname{tr}T=\sum_{g\in G}\operatorname{tr}(D_1(g)BD_1(g)^{-1})=|G|\operatorname{tr}B.
$$
Since $\operatorname{tr}(cI_n)=nc$, we obtain
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{|G|\operatorname{tr}B}{n}I_n.}
$$

