= Solution
{parent=i}

A <simple module> is nonzero and has no proper nonzero <submodule>. A <composition series of a module> is a finite strict chain $0=V_0<V_1<\cdots<V_n=V$ of <submodules> with simple quotients $V_i/V_{i-1}$. These quotients are the <composition factors> of the series.

The <Jordan–Hölder theorem for modules> says that if a <module> has a composition series, then any two such series have the same length and the same simple factors up to <isomorphism> and reordering. In particular, every finite-dimensional <module> has a well-defined multiset of composition factors.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose $s\ne0$ in the <simple module> $S$. The map $\phi:A\to S$, $a\mapsto as$, is an $A$-module homomorphism whose <image> is the nonzero <submodule> $As$, hence all of $S$. Thus the <first isomorphism theorem for modules> gives
$$
\boxed{S\cong A/\ker\phi.}
$$
The kernel is a maximal left ideal because the quotient is simple. Whenever the regular <module> has finite length, as it does for a finite-dimensional <algebra>, take a composition series of the kernel and append $\ker\phi<A$. Its last factor is $S$, so $S$ occurs as a <composition factor> of the regular <module>; the <Jordan–Hölder theorem for modules> makes that independent of the chosen series.

The quotient conclusion holds for every unital <algebra>. If an arbitrary infinite-dimensional regular <module> has no finite composition series, the universally valid statement is that $S$ is a simple subquotient; a claim about the factors of a finite series requires finite length.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A <semisimple module> is a direct sum of <simple modules>. An <algebra> is semisimple when all its <modules> are semisimple. Suppose the left regular <module> $A$ is semisimple. Every <module> $M$ is a quotient of a free <module> $\bigoplus_{j\in J}A$, by sending its basis elements to a generating set of $M$. This free <module> is a direct sum of simple <modules>.

A quotient of a direct sum of simple <modules> is semisimple: the images of its simple summands are simple or zero and span the quotient. Choose a maximal direct sum among these simple images, allowing all simple submodules in the choice. If it does not span the whole quotient, some simple image is not contained in it. Its intersection with that direct sum is zero by simplicity, so it could be added, contradicting maximality. For infinite index sets, <Zorn's lemma> supplies the maximal direct sum; in finite dimension the construction terminates directly.

Hence every $M$ is semisimple, and **$A$ is a <semisimple algebra>**. This proves <semisimplicity from the regular module>.

