{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

By <Lagrange's theorem>, a nonidentity element in a group of order four has order two or four. In the latter case the group is cyclic. Otherwise every nonidentity element has order two, and $(ab)^{-1}=b^{-1}a^{-1}=ba$ while $(ab)^{-1}=ab$, so the group is <Abelian>. Choose distinct nonidentity elements $a,b$. Their cyclic subgroups meet trivially and $ab$ is the fourth element, so $(i,j)\mapsto a^ib^j$ gives an <isomorphism> $C_2\times C_2\to G$. Hence
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_4\quad\text{or}\quad C_2\times C_2.}
$$
No classification of finite <Abelian groups> is used.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By <Cauchy theorem for groups>, choose $a$ of order $p$ and $b$ of order two. The subgroup $P=\langle a\rangle$ has index two, hence is normal, and $b\notin P$. Thus $a,b$ generate $G$. Conjugation by $b$ has $bab^{-1}=a^r$ with $r\not\equiv0\pmod p$ and $r^2\equiv1\pmod p$. Since $p$ is prime, $r\equiv1$ or $-1$.

If $r=1$, the two generators commute and $ab$ has order $2p$, so $G$ is cyclic. If $r=-1$, then $(ab)^2=e$ and $ab\ne b$. The two involutions $b,ab$ generate $G$, because $(ab)b=a$. Hence $G$ is dihedral in the stated convention. Thus
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_{2p}\quad\text{or}\quad D_{2p},}
$$
where $D_{2p}$ denotes the dihedral group of order $2p$. In both cases $P$ is a proper nontrivial <normal subgroup>, so **$G$ is not simple**.

= 5
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For a finite <group> with $|G|=p^am$ and $p\nmid m$, a <Sylow p-subgroup> has order $p^a$. The <Sylow theorems> assert existence, containment of every <p-subgroup> in a Sylow subgroup, conjugacy of all Sylow subgroups, and $n_p\mid m$, $n_p\equiv1\pmod p$.

For <Sylow subgroups of A6>, $|A_6|=6!/2=360=2^3\cdot3^2\cdot5$. Explicit choices are
$$
\boxed{P_5=\langle(12345)\rangle\cong C_5,\qquad P_3=\langle(123),(456)\rangle\cong C_3\times C_3.}
$$
Their elements are even permutations, and their orders are the largest powers of five and three dividing $360$.

Every nonidentity element of $P_5$ is a five-cycle. There are $\binom65\,4!=144$ five-cycles, and distinct subgroups of order five meet only at the identity. Each contributes four such elements, giving
$$
\boxed{n_5=144/4=36.}
$$
For $P_3$, each unordered partition of the six letters into two triples gives the subgroup generated by the two triple-cycles. It is independent of the chosen orientation of either cycle. There are $\tfrac12\binom63=10$ such partitions. Different partitions give different subgroups, since the four single three-cycles in a subgroup identify the two triples. Every Sylow subgroup of order nine is conjugate to the displayed $P_3$, so every one arises from a partition in this way. Hence
$$
\boxed{n_3=10.}
$$
The <normalizer> is $N_G(H)=\{g\in G:gHg^{-1}=H\}$. Its definition makes $H$ normal in $N_G(H)$. If $H$ is a <Sylow subgroup> of $G$, it is also a Sylow subgroup of $N_G(H)$, since $H\leq N_G(H)\leq G$ leaves no room for a larger power of $p$. The <Sylow theorems> make all Sylow subgroups of the normalizer conjugate there, and normality of $H$ makes every such conjugate equal to $H$. Thus **$H$ is the unique Sylow subgroup of its normalizer**.

