= Solution
{parent=a}

The <commutator subgroup> is $G'=\langle xyx^{-1}y^{-1}:x,y\in G\rangle$. It is characteristic and hence normal, because every automorphism sends a commutator to a commutator. If $G'\leq H$, then $ghg^{-1}h^{-1}\in H$ for every $g\in G,h\in H$, so $H$ is normal. Every commutator becomes trivial in $G/H$, making that quotient <Abelian>. Conversely, the quotient homomorphism to an <Abelian> group kills each commutator, and hence kills $G'$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G'\leq H\iff H\triangleleft G\text{ and }G/H\text{ is Abelian}.}
$$
Set $G^{(0)}=G$, $G^{(i+1)}=(G^{(i)})'$. The <group> is <soluble> exactly when this <derived series> reaches the identity after finitely many steps.

If a nontrivial simple <group> is soluble, its normal subgroup $G'$ is either $1$ or $G$. The latter would make the derived series constant and nontrivial, so $G'=1$ and $G$ is <Abelian>. Every nonidentity element then generates $G$, since its cyclic subgroup is normal. An infinite cyclic group is not simple, and a finite cyclic group is simple exactly when its order is prime. This proves <soluble simple group has prime order>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

An <Abelian group> has the central series $1<G$, since $Z(G)=G$. In any central series, each factor is a subgroup of a center and hence <Abelian>. A finite normal series with <Abelian> factors makes the group <soluble>: inductively, if $N$ is soluble and $G/N$ is <Abelian>, then $G'\subseteq N$, and successive derived groups eventually vanish. Hence **every nilpotent group is soluble**.

The group $S_3$ has the normal series $1<A_3<S_3$ with cyclic factors of orders three and two, so it is soluble. Its center is trivial, since a permutation commuting with both $(12)$ and $(23)$ must fix all three letters. Every nontrivial nilpotent group has nontrivial center: its first nonidentity central-series term is contained in $Z(G)$. Consequently **$S_3$ is soluble but not nilpotent**.

For a finite <p-group>, use induction on its order. The identity group is the base case. A nontrivial $G$ has nontrivial $Z=Z(G)$, so $G/Z$ has smaller order, still a power of $p$. By induction it has a central series. Lift that series to subgroups of $G$ and prepend $1<Z$. The first factor is central by definition of $Z$, and centrality of every lifted factor is exactly centrality of the corresponding quotient factor. This gives a central series for $G$. This proves <finite p-groups are nilpotent>:
$$
\boxed{\text{Every finite p-group is nilpotent}.}
$$
